{"records":[{"id":"t6g8bfpgvk","title":"M. Jost et al,. Mol Cell 2017 - Figure 5D - Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent","description":"Microscopy files used for the analysis of data presented in figure 5D of\r\nM. Jost et al., Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent. 2017","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-08-29T10:28:07.227Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t6g8bfpgvk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t6g8bfpgvk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lenno","last_name":"Krenning","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ankit","last_name":"Rai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Akhmanova","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","In Vitro Techniques"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"dfdxy4ksh3","title":"Figure 4 - Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent","description":"Microscopy files used for the analysis of data presented in figures 4 and S4 of\r\nM. Jost et al., Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent. 2017","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-07-18T06:50:43.254Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dfdxy4ksh3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dfdxy4ksh3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lenno","last_name":"Krenning","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marvin","last_name":"Tanenbaum","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Microscopy"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"tm22dtht9x","title":"M. Jost et al,. Mol Cell 2017 - Figure 5A - Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent","description":"Microscopy files used for the analysis of data presented in figure 5A of\r\nM. Jost et al., Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent. Mol. Cell. 2017","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-08-29T10:27:52.938Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tm22dtht9x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tm22dtht9x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lenno","last_name":"Krenning","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","In Vitro Techniques"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nw9yp22mfk","title":"RNAseq analysis results","description":"Results obtained from transcriptional profiling of genes of the isolated yeast strains using RNAseq analysis. Excel file with differential expression of genes and counts per million of yeast genes for the studied strains","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-09T07:42:04.594Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nw9yp22mfk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nw9yp22mfk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"DESPINA","last_name":"LOLA","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Differential Gene Expression","Wine Yeasts"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xfhwgr5vh7","title":"Effects of Dietary Sterol-Enriched Yeast Hydrolysate on Ovar-ian Development, Reproductive Endocrine Status, Im-mune-Inflammatory Responses, Antioxidant Indices, and Egg Quality in Late-Laying Yellow-Feathered Broiler Breeder Hens","description":"The present study investigated the effects of graded dietary supplementation with sterol-enriched yeast hydrolysate (SYH) on reproductive performance, egg quality, ovarian development, reproductive endocrine status, immune-inflammatory responses, antioxidant status, and ovarian gene expression in late-laying yellow-feathered broiler breeder hens. A total of 720 55-week-old hens were assigned to four dietary treatments containing 0, 0.2, 0.5, or 0.8 g/kg SYH and were evaluated over a six-week feeding period.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-03T13:28:05.546Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xfhwgr5vh7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xfhwgr5vh7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haoran","last_name":"Si","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Animal Reproduction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"27tj2crsw7","title":"Figures 1-3 for \"Elevated levels of the sphingolipid MIPC disrupt the coordination of cell growth with cell wall formation in fission yeast\"","description":"Main figures 1-3 from \"Elevated levels of the sphingolipid MIPC disrupt the coordination of cell growth with cell wall formation in fission yeast.\" The data from experiments are live-cell imaging, imaging quantification, lipodomics and yeast growth assays.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-31T22:54:39.156Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/27tj2crsw7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/27tj2crsw7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alaina","last_name":"Willet","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04q48ey07","name":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grant_id":"R35GM131799"}],"categories":["Genetics","Microscopy"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":105694,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":121,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":105694,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":105252,"restricted":53,"embargoed":389},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1855},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":549},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":422},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":359},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":358},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Develo